{% extends 'help/article.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
    <p>
        Permissions here are a <strong>role plus adjustments</strong>. The role does the heavy
        lifting, and anything it gets wrong for one person you fix on that person — without
        inventing a new role or giving someone more than they need.
    </p>

    <h2>Inviting someone</h2>
    <p>Under Users &amp; Access you can add a person two ways:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Set a password yourself</strong> and pass it on.</li>
        <li><strong>Have the app email an invitation</strong> with a generated password.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Either way they get a role immediately. An email address can only be used once in your
        company, so a duplicate invitation is refused rather than creating a second account.
    </p>

    <h2>The four roles</h2>
    <div class="help-scroll">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Role</th><th>Intended for</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Administrator</strong></td><td>Complete access, including users, billing and integrations.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Operations Manager</strong></td><td>Runs the fleet day to day. Everything operational, but no user administration and no integration keys.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Dispatcher</strong></td><td>Books and dispatches loads. Sees drivers and equipment, but not profit and not expense amounts.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Driver</strong></td><td>Defined for the driver portal, which has not shipped. Not yet useful for office staff.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Access levels</h2>
    <p>Within a role, each area of the app sits at one of four levels:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>No access</strong> — the page is hidden entirely. Not greyed out, not an error on click: it is not in their navigation.</li>
        <li><strong>View</strong> — can read.</li>
        <li><strong>Manage</strong> — can create and edit.</li>
        <li><strong>Full</strong> — adds the destructive and administrative actions. Permanent deletion is usually what separates Full from Manage.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Adjusting one person</h2>
    <p>
        Open a user and you see their role's defaults with every individual permission listed. From
        there you can <strong>grant</strong> something the role does not include, or
        <strong>revoke</strong> something it does.
    </p>
    <p>Typical cases:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Give one operations manager <code>drivers.view_ssn</code> because they handle onboarding — and nobody else.</li>
        <li>Revoke delete from a new dispatcher for their first few months.</li>
        <li>Give a dispatcher profit visibility because they also quote.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Adjustments sit on top of the role. Change someone's role later and the adjustments still
        apply, so review them when you promote somebody.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
        <p>You cannot edit your own permissions, and you cannot disable your own account or the last
        remaining administrator. Those guards exist so nobody can lock the company out.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Hidden fields are absent, not blanked</h2>
    <p>
        This is the part worth understanding properly. When someone lacks permission for a
        <em>field</em> — an SSN, a driver's pay, a load's rate, an expense amount — that field is
        <strong>left out of the data sent to their browser entirely</strong>. It is not rendered
        and then hidden with styling, and it is not sent as an empty value.
    </p>
    <p>
        So a dispatcher who opens developer tools still cannot read a rate. There is nothing there
        to find. The four fields protected this way are:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Driver SSN, and the SSN card image</li>
        <li>Driver pay details</li>
        <li>Load rate</li>
        <li>Expense amounts</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The same applies to totals: expense and vendor summaries have the money stripped out for a
        user who cannot see amounts, rather than being computed and hidden.
    </p>

    <h2>Change history</h2>
    <p>
        Edits to drivers, loads, trucks, trailers, users and the company are recorded field by
        field, with who made them. It is how you answer "who changed this rate" — and how a
        column that someone accidentally cleared can be traced back to what it used to hold.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
